retrieval_text_enriched:
The Central Goods and Services Tax Act, 2017. Chapter XV: DEMANDS AND RECOVERY. Section 79: Recovery of tax.

Summary: If a person fails to pay any amount due under the GST Act or its rules, the proper officer may recover the amount by deduction, detention and sale of goods, issuing a notice to third parties for payment, distraining property, certifying the debt to the Collector, or filing an application before a magistrate; the officer may amend or revoke notices, and specific provisions apply to bonds, State/UT taxes, and proportional crediting of recovered sums.

Topics: Recovery of unpaid tax | Modes of tax recovery | Notice to third parties | Distrain and sale of property | Certification to Collector | Magistrate proceedings

Keywords: proper officer | deduction | detention | sale | notice | defaulter | bond | State tax | Union territory tax | magistrate

Applies to: any person liable to pay tax, interest or penalty under the GST Act or its rules

Conditions: amount payable under the Act or rules is not paid | notice under sub‑clause (i) is issued to a person holding money for the defaulter | property is under the control of the proper officer or specified officer

Exceptions: if the person served with notice proves that the money demanded was not due to the defaulter or will not become due, the notice does not require payment (sub‑clause (vii))

Obligations: proper officer must proceed to recover the unpaid amount | person receiving notice must comply with the notice | person making payment under notice is deemed to have paid on behalf of the defaulter

