retrieval_text_enriched:
The Central Goods and Services Tax Act, 2017. Chapter XIX: OFFENCES AND PENALTIES. Section 122: Penalty for certain offences.

Summary: Section 122 prescribes penalties for taxable persons and other persons who commit a wide range of offences such as issuing false invoices, failing to pay collected tax, not deducting or collecting tax, misusing input tax credit, fraudulent refund claims, false information, obstruction of officers, unregistered supplies, and aiding such offences, with penalties ranging from ten thousand rupees to amounts equivalent to the tax evaded or up to twenty‑five thousand rupees, subject to specific conditions.

Topics: Offences | Penalties | GST invoicing | Tax collection and payment | Input tax credit | Registration | Electronic commerce operators | Aiding and abetting | Confiscation of goods

Keywords: penalty | taxable person | invoice | false invoice | tax evasion | input tax credit | refund | registration | obstruction | confiscation | electronic commerce operator | aid and abet

Applies to: taxable persons | registered persons | electronic commerce operators | any person who aids or abets the listed offences | persons dealing with goods liable to confiscation | persons dealing with services in contravention of the Act | persons failing to appear before a tax officer

Conditions: supplies goods or services without issuing an invoice | issues an incorrect or false invoice | issues an invoice without supply of goods or services | collects tax but fails to pay it to the Government beyond three months from due date | fails to deduct tax as required under section 51 | fails to collect tax as required under section 52 | takes or utilizes input tax credit without receipt of goods or services | fraudulently obtains a refund of tax | takes or distributes input tax credit contrary to section 20 | falsifies financial records or furnishes false information to evade tax | fails to obtain registration when liable | furnishes false information regarding registration particulars | obstructs or prevents any officer in discharge of duties | transports taxable goods without required documents | suppresses turnover leading to tax evasion | fails to keep or retain books of account as required | fails to furnish information or documents to an officer or furnishes false information | supplies, transports or stores goods believed to be liable to confiscation | issues invoice using another person's registration number | tamper with or destroy material evidence or documents | dispose of or tamper with detained, seized or attached goods

Exceptions: where the penalty under clause (2)(a) applies, the amount is ten thousand rupees or ten percent of tax due, whichever is higher, if the reason is not fraud or wilful misstatement | where the reason is fraud or wilful misstatement, the penalty under clause (2)(b) is ten thousand rupees or the tax due, whichever is higher

Obligations: pay a penalty of ten thousand rupees or an amount equivalent to the tax evaded, whichever is higher | pay a penalty of ten thousand rupees or ten percent of tax due, whichever is higher, for non‑fraudulent reasons under clause (2)(a) | pay a penalty of ten thousand rupees or the tax due, whichever is higher, for fraudulent reasons under clause (2)(b) | pay a penalty up to twenty‑five thousand rupees for aiding or abetting offences or related conduct under clause (3)

Procedural steps: issue a summons for appearance before a central tax officer | appear before the officer when summoned | furnish information or documents as required by an officer | maintain books of account and other documents as prescribed

Deadlines: payment of collected tax must be made within three months from the date it becomes due

Thresholds: minimum penalty of ten thousand rupees | penalty may be ten percent of tax due under clause (2)(a) | penalty may be the full tax due under clause (2)(b) | maximum penalty of twenty‑five thousand rupees for offences under clause (3)

Questions: What penalty is prescribed for issuing a false invoice? | How long can a taxable person delay payment of collected tax before a penalty applies? | What is the penalty for an electronic commerce operator that allows an unregistered person to supply goods through its platform? | When does the penalty increase to the amount of tax due instead of ten thousand rupees? | What are the consequences for failing to appear before a tax officer after being summoned?

